<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Ukrainian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Ukrainian War was fought from November 1918 to July 1919 over Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, after the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. It began with fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> between Polish residents and the forces of the West Ukrainian People's Republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> won, occupied all of Eastern Galicia and the Lemko lands, and the Ukrainian government and army withdrew across the Zbruch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-ukrainian-war.jpg" alt="Polish–Ukrainian War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Дмитро Вортман, Максим Майоров, Леонід Криницький · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:02._%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE-%D1%83%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%97%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D1%96%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_1918-1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war had roots in the ethnic, cultural and political differences between the Polish and Ukrainian populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. The relative leniency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> towards national minorities let both Polish and Ukrainian national movements develop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. After the 1848 Revolution the Austrians, concerned by Polish demands for greater autonomy, supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, the East Slavic people later recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>. The Ruthenian intelligentsia adopted the name Ukrainians, and in 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Ukrainians held separate conferences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>. The two movements developed with contradictory goals, which caused the later clash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 18 October 1918 a Ukrainian National Council was formed, and during the night of 31 October to 1 November Ukrainian units of 1,400 soldiers and 60 officers, led by Captain Dmytro Vitovsky, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>. The West Ukrainian People's Republic was proclaimed on 13 November 1918, with Lviv as its capital. Because the republic was not recognised internationally and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s boundaries had not yet been defined, the question was reduced to military control. Polish self-defence units held out until a relief force broke through on 21 November. Polish troops and criminals then looted the Jewish and Ukrainian quarters, killing up to 340 civilians.</p><p>Ukrainian forces, with a large numerical advantage until February 1919, held most of Eastern Galicia until May. A Polish offensive began on 14 May 1919 with the Blue Army of General Józef Haller, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> opened a second front on 23 May. A Ukrainian counter-offensive, the Chortkiv Offensive, under Oleksander Hrekov began on 8 June and came within two days' march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, but it ran short of ammunition, with only five to ten bullets for each soldier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> won the disputed territory on 18 July 1919. Both sides interned civilians in camps, where many died of cold, hunger and typhoid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, mostly soldiers, died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a> and, to a lesser extent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> opposed Polish expansion, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> with arms, officers and Haller's army. France later obtained control of the Eastern Galician oil fields. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and after the war the region remained within Soviet Ukraine.</p><p>Although the 70 to 75 thousand men of the Ukrainian Galician Army lost, the experience of proclaiming a state and fighting for it deepened Ukrainian nationalism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in Eastern Galicia, the victory ended 146 years of foreign domination, and the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, including the young defenders of the Łyczakowski Cemetery, was long remembered as an example of Polish heroism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Ukrainian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…were won in a series of wars fought by the new Polish Army from 1918 to 1921: the <b>Polish–Ukrainian War</b> from Lwów, the Greater Poland uprising, the Polish–Czechoslovak War over Trans-Olza, the Polish–Lithuanian War, three Silesian Uprisings and, most critical, the Polish–Soviet War (1919–1921), which ended in a…</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1919 Polish troops entered the area during the <b>Polish-Ukrainian War</b> and dissolved the republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/komancza-republic">Komancza Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Polish residents of Lviv resisted, and the <b>Polish-Ukrainian War</b> began; the Ukrainians lost Lviv in November and moved the capital to Ternopil and then Stanislaviv.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
